From 233a3cff345d7a6381a8203569f0ff60ea417593 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Charliechen114514 <725610365@qq.com>
Date: Sat, 15 Aug 2026 22:12:34 
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: rk3588-topeet: cap big clusters at 2.208 GHz (diagnostic)

With 0012's defer-safe registration the CPU DVFS path finally runs, and the
board now wedges (all-CPU, IRQ+NMI silent, DW-watchdog reset) on every boot
at GNOME/GDM load — the formerly-rare hang made deterministic.  vendor gates
the 2.4 GHz OPP to top speed-bin silicon (PVTM/binning, intentionally not
ported); our table exposes it ungated at 1.0 V.

NOTE: the ladder nodes live in the included rk3588-opp.dtsi; 0011's board
hunks only override their properties (dual-supply voltages).  Removing the
board-level 2.4 override alone therefore leaves the base single-supply OPP
active — the cap MUST be /delete-node/ on the referenced tables.

Diagnostic A/B: cap both big clusters at 2.208 GHz @ 987.5 mV (also the
vendor high-temperature clamp).  Stable board => rail/binning theory
confirmed => port proper bin-gating or keep the cap.  Still wedging =>
next suspects: the DSU coupling gap or the i2c/RK860X domain.

---
diff --git a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-topeet.dts b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-topeet.dts
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-topeet.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/rockchip/rk3588-topeet.dts
@@ -219,11 +219,7 @@
 		opp-microvolt = <987500 987500 1000000>,
 				<987500 987500 1000000>;
 	};
-
-	opp-2400000000 {
-		opp-microvolt = <1000000 1000000 1000000>,
-				<1000000 1000000 1000000>;
-	};
+	/delete-node/ opp-2400000000;
 };
 
 &cluster2_opp_table {
@@ -267,11 +263,7 @@
 		opp-microvolt = <987500 987500 1000000>,
 				<987500 987500 1000000>;
 	};
-
-	opp-2400000000 {
-		opp-microvolt = <1000000 1000000 1000000>,
-				<1000000 1000000 1000000>;
-	};
+	/delete-node/ opp-2400000000;
 };
 
 /* === CPU supply (modeled on fet3588-c; big clusters share one rk8602 rail for cpu+mem) === */
